{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790912415,"nonce":"9YcF3CfsQ6YPuGdLPuthMQmZ9eo=","message":"**Owner's ruling (2026-10-02): the model provider is generic. This comment supersedes the body.**\n\n**The decision.** The Investigator talks to models through one port, `ModelProvider`, and the first adapter speaks the **OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions API with tool calling**. One adapter, configured by base URL, model name and a sealed API key, reaches any provider or gateway that serves that API (hosted APIs, Azure, OpenRouter, LiteLLM, vLLM, Ollama). No provider's name appears in domain code. A native adapter for any one vendor is a later widening behind the same port, justified only by a tool-calling fidelity gap shown in a test.\n\n**Shape.**\n- The port takes messages + Tool schemas and returns a turn (text and/or tool calls) plus **token usage**. Usage is mandatory, because ADR 0053 §6's token budgets are enforced from it; an adapter that cannot report usage fails a run as `failed`, it does not run unbudgeted.\n- The Investigator's version pins `(base URL identity, model name)`; changing either is a new version (ADR 0053 §6).\n- The API key is sealed like a channel credential (`channel_credentials` pattern) and never appears in a Step.\n- A **fake provider** (scripted turns) is part of this ticket — it is what makes `180a525`'s criteria testable without a network.\n\n**Done when.** The port, the OpenAI-compatible adapter and the fake exist; the adapter is tested against a recorded tool-calling exchange; a missing-usage response fails the run; settings can store a provider config with a sealed key.\n\n**Blocked by.** None.\n\n**Mode.** AFK.","files":null}]}